In article <31519d$fbb@nova.netapp.com> hitz@nova.netapp.com (Dave Hitz) writes:

   You can't throw an extra disk into the array if you number blocks in a
   rotating pattern across the disks, but if you number blocks down one
   entire disk, and then down the next entire disk, then adding a new disk
   simply means that you extend the range of available blocks by the size
   of the new disk.

   Dave Hitz		hitz@netapp.com
   Network Appliance 	(415) 428-5106


One problem with numbering blocks down one disk, is that you can't
take advantage of the parallel bandwidth when reading multiple blocks.
For example, if reading sequential blocks, and blocks are numbered
across the disks, you can read all the disks at once to get the data.
Basically, RAID3 at a much higher grain size.  The other advantage is
that for large writes across disks, the parity block can be written at
the same time as the data instead of doing the normal pre reads to
generate the parity.

Of course, the bandwidth problem may not be an issue if the
interconnect can't handle the extra bandwidth.  Also, most
applications are mostly single block access anyway, so it may not be
an issue.  But high bandwidths apps, like video and supercomputing, do
a lot of multi-block accesses.  Anyway, just something else to think
about, when designing your RAID system.

John Chandy

