In article <1994Jul26.151823.4463@malad1.mala.bc.ca>, dunnett@mala.bc.ca (Malcolm Dunnett) writes:
|> 
|>   I'm looking for information on the function of the jumpers on the front
|> of an RZ26 disk drive.
|> original RZ26, not the "L" variant ).


Hold the drive right-side up, with the little row of pins facing you.  They
should be on the left of the board.  Pin 1 is on the lower-left.  Pin 2 is
immediately above, etc.

From the left, the first three sets are the target ID.

The next row are /FAULT_SINK, and a key (missing pin).  Connect your LED's
cathode to this pin to monitor "fault" conditions.  Obviously the anode
goes to +5 through a resisitor.

Next on the left from /FAULT is the /BUSY pin you're looking for (pin 7).

To the left of that are a +5 line and a reserved pin above.

Next is the delayed spin jumper position.  Shorting this causes the drive
to spin up when power is applied.  Removing it will cause the drive to spin
up only in response to a "start unit" command (unless you've enabled the
"staggered spin up" feature in the special function control page, in which
case the drive will spin up after waiting for a certain amount of time,
based on its target ID.)

