danj@hub.parallan.com (Dan Jones) writes:

>hitz@nova.netapp.com (Dave Hitz) writes:

>> 
>> I disagree with the implication that RAID 5 is necessarily "better" than
>> RAID 4.  I think it makes more sense to stick with RAID 4 and solve the
>> hot-parity problem with better write allocation patterns in the file
>> system.  The benefit is that you can easily add new disks to the end of
>> a RAID 4 array by simply zeroing them.  Expanding a RAID 5 array is
>> tricky.
>>
>Interesting point, but I would think that it would be just as tricky
>to make the new RAID4 drive useable; in as much as all of the data
>should be reformatted. I don't know that it is better to have good
>parity covering uninitialized data versus bad parity covering the
>same case as in a RAID5. Something would still have to keep track
>of the status of the new drive with respect to the array.


>Dan Jones
>Parallan Computer, Inc       "Now..., you tell me what you know." G. Marx

As a matter of possible interest IBM's 9337 RAID 5 product for their AS/400 computers
allows addition of more drives after starting with some minimum number
and it does not require reformatting nor rearranging the existing data which
stays on the drives where it was. I presume that the new drives are formatted
to all zeros so as not to disturb existing parity.

Their trick is to rotate parity only across the minimal drive configuration.
It is my understanding that the AS/400 won't do sequential I/O anyway so there
is no reason to optimize for it. To my knowledge IBM was granted the first
patent for a RAID 5 storage subsystem but it was invented in their Rochester, MN
plant where they design AS/400s so they did not include the sequential striping
throughput mechanisms in the patent. 

I would think that you could avoid some problems by numbering your blocks  1 - M
across the N drives where N <= M. That is just skip the numbers for uninstalled
drives - they can be used if those drive slots are ever populated. Your RAID
control would need to be careful to keep track of which 'new' blocks had been 
integrated with existing stripes and which are mapped to other uses. But 
mappings have been getting more versatile (and complex) lately.

