In article <CtKCFM.7F7@park.uvsc.edu>, Malcolm Weir <malc@park.uvsc.edu> wrote:
>No it isn't: you can do exactly the same with a RAID 5 base as with a RAID 4
>one, by simply adding zeroed drives to the end of the array.  

Once you start doing this, it isn't true RAID 5 any more, but your
point is well taken that it's better than RAID 4.

> (Which would you rather have: one hot, or several warm?).

If you can keep all of your writes right next to each other, which is
the point of log based file systems like LFS (and WAFL which is our
file system), then the parity disk isn't hot anyway, so you don't have
to choose.

On the other hand, with a true RAID 5 or your RAID 4/5 hybrid, you can
theoretically get full read bandwidth from all N disks at once, instead
of N-1 with RAID 4.  Thus on raw read throughput, RAID 5 or hybrid 4/5
has a 1/N advantage over RAID 4.

