In article <RPB.94Jul25150438@axp01.ox.ac.uk>,
Ray Bellis <rpb@psy.ox.ac.uk> wrote:
>I've noticed a misfeature in the DEC OSF/1 `ps' command:
>
>If you use the `ps' command, the width of the output is automatically
>truncated to the width of the terminal *even* if the output isn't
>going to the terminal.
>
>Thus, if you do `ps aux | grep cron' on an 80 column terminal it
>won't find your `cron' process.
>
>DEC, please consider turning this terminal width feature off when
>the stdout isn't a tty.
>
>Ray.

At least under OSF/1 Version 2.0, if you use the 'w' flag, then this
won't happen.

From the ps man page:
  w   Uses a wide output format (132 columns (bytes) rather than 80); if this
      flag is doubled (ww), uses an arbitrarily wide output.  This informa-
      tion determines how much of long commands to print.

And it even works!
<1:47pm>renoir/gallatin:~>ps aux | grep cron
gallatin 22268  0.0  0.1 1.30M 288K p7  S  + 13:47:53     0:00.02 grep cron
<1:47pm>renoir/gallatin:~>ps auxww | grep cron
root     16518  0.0  0.0 1.35M  24K ??  I      Jul 20     0:00.28 /usr/sbin/cron
gallatin 22270  0.0  0.1 1.30M 288K p7  S  + 13:47:59     0:00.03 grep cron


