In article <30rq92$bet@nova.netapp.com> hitz@nova.netapp.com (Dave Hitz) writes:
>
>I disagree with the implication that RAID 5 is necessarily "better" than
>RAID 4.  I think it makes more sense to stick with RAID 4 and solve the
>hot-parity problem with better write allocation patterns in the file
>system.  The benefit is that you can easily add new disks to the end of
>a RAID 4 array by simply zeroing them.  Expanding a RAID 5 array is
>tricky.

No it isn't: you can do exactly the same with a RAID 5 base as with a RAID 4
one, by simply adding zeroed drives to the end of the array.  This increases
the complexity slightly, since you now need to consider two stripe widths
depending on whether you're dealing with data or parity, and results in
a slightly "warmer" set of drives (containing the parity) than your expansion
drives, but the hybrid result is still an improvement over a pure RAID 4
scheme, which be definition has a hot disk for parity!  (Which would you
rather have: one hot, or several warm?).

