tvmyers@koicds01.icdc.delcoelect.com (Thomas V. Myers) writes:

>Malcolm Weir (malc@park.uvsc.edu) wrote:

>> Its actually not even smart enough to be a RAID _5_ system: its really a
>> RAID _4_ system with cache and a CPU and firmware (Coo!  New Technology!!!!).

>I guess I've been living in a cave, but I've only heard of RAID systems
>numbered 0 (simple disk striping), 1 (mirrored), 3 (dedicated parity) and 5
>(scattered parity).  What's 4 (or 7 for that matter, no flames please)?

>--
>Tom Myers : tvmyers@icdc.delcoelect.com

RAID 4 is also dedicated parity but the striping unit is larger than RAID 3.
RAID 3 stripes bits or bytes or words or something less than sectors across
the disks while RAID 4 stripes sectors or larger units. RAID 3 usually uses
a multiplexer/demux to join/split the streams to the disk drives while RAID 4
and 5 just arrange the blocks adjacently in buffer/cache or just transmit in
correct order to host.

RAID 7 is not defined or multiply defined. Mylex uses the RAID 7 term to mean
not an array or Just a Bunch of Disks (JBOD) while Storage Computer means
a RAID 4 with per device buffers and on-board firmware. RAID 0 - 6 designate
(or should) defined architectural modes while RAID 7 is Marketecture - another
noise word that has been misappropriated.
-- 
