From article <30m7l9$1df@uuneo.neosoft.com>,
by jleslie@dmccorp.com (Jerry Leslie):
> 
>      Tadpole Technology designs and manufactures workstation-class
> portable computer systems  ...

I own one, a Tadpole SPARCbook 1, and I like it, even though Tadpole
considers it an obsolete product.  They are now pushing their
SPARCbook 3 product family now, and when last I checked, they had
a fair inventory of "obsolete" earlier models priced at $2500 each.

Typical of all the SPARCbooks, and I assume the Alpha laptop will be
very similar, are built in SCSI, Ethernet, parallel port, serial
port, fax/modem, external display, external keyboard and mouse, and
DC power jack.  Merely finding spaces for all of those plugs on the
back is a chore, considering the small size of the machine.

The newer models also have external battery for longer field time,
sterio audio, ISDN compatability, PCMIA slot, and removable internal
SCSI hard drive.

Tadpole uses an isometric mouse key on their SPARCbooks.  Early models
had it off to the side, but on the new model, it is in the triangle
between the B, G and H keys, as on the new IBM laptops.  I'm not sure
which I prefer, but isometric mice work quite well, once you get used
to them.

> Q.  How much will the Alpha AXP notebook cost?

As I already noted, Tadpole's older SPARCbooks can be had for $2500;
this gets you about 8 meg and a color display.  Last time I checked,
current models were going for more like $4000 and up, depending on how
you configure them.  They've got lots of configuration options -- you
can buy a small memory configuration, giving longer battery life but
a bit of thrashing, or you can avoid thrashing by stuffing it with
memory.  Similarly, there are various ways you can configure your
disks -- do you want floppy support, or just hard disk.

I suspect that the Alpha laptop will be similar in the option range
they support, and I see no reason to expect the pricing to be radically
different.  Expect prices in the range of $4K to $14K, depending on
options, with a new model to be released 1 to 2 years later, and fire
sale prices on old Alpha notebooks falling to the $2500 level, where
they will remain stable for perhaps 3 more years.

In any case, what's the name going to be?  The Sun's SPARC is available
as a SPARCbook.  The Powerbook is on the market too, so will it be called
the Alphabook (or perhaps a-book, with the a replaced by a greek alpha?).
Isn't Alphabok the trademark of some game sold by Milton Bradley?  Could
that cause a problem?

		Doug Jones
		(I am not a spokesman for Tadpole or DEC, just a customer!)

		jones@cs.uiowa.edu


