In article <2vqcg0$hhh@st-james.comp.vuw.ac.nz>, dmdj@comms.moc.govt.nz (Mike Jensen) writes:
> 
> CAN I USE DECserver-90M TERM SERVERS OVER SLIP - RELIABLY
> 
> I am in the middle of upgrading old terminal servers 
> (DS200-MC, DS200-DL and DS300 models).  I have DEChub 90
> units available to accomodate new units)
> 
> Can I use a DECserver 90M to reliably connect from home to work
> using SLIP?  (these units only have limited modem control).  The
> Systems and Options book says the unit supports SLIP, but the unit
> only has limited modem control due to it being RJ45-terminated.
> The DECserver 700 (8-port) units support full modem control, so
> I assume no problem with SLIP connections.
> I have heard that "limited" modem control may mean problems with
> modems which do not operate at a fixed speed, ie they simply run
> as fast as they can and use compression to improve throughput.
> I plan to operate this type of modem and replace my existing fixed
> speed units (2400bps - pathetic!).

I have successfully configured DECserver 90TL units for dialup and
outcall usage in conjunction with US Robotics Sportster 14400 modems.
The 90TL is also characterized as "limited modem control",
with RJ45 jacks (MJ8) as well.

I am using XON-XOFF flow control, so the flow control is "in-band".
It is my understanding that the SLIP protocol needs to use
out of band flow control, usually CTS/RTS signalling.

The limited modem control products only have 1 input and one
output signal for modem handshaking.  If you are already
using those for dialup/hangup signalling, there are none left
for out of band flow control.  This would seem to preclude
a viable dialup SLIP implementation.

The true irony is that the limited modem control products
seems to have the hardware to support the full MJ8 signalling,
but the controlware doesn't talk to the hardware.
Probably a marketing decision.



