In article <30i44s$4us@newserv.ksu.ksu.edu>, electro@godiva
(Eric L Patterson) writes:

|>What are the boards involved in installing more than one processer in a 
|>MVII

  Assuming you mean plugging a second KA630 processor into the backplane,
  you will have to reset the second KA630 to a non-arbiter (and to the
  first of three alternate CSR/IPCR addresses), and plug it in to the
  Q-bus.  The arbiter IPCR is at 20001F40, and the three auxillaries are
  located at 1F42, 44, and 46.  (These addresses are for the KA655 system;
  I think these also hold for the KA630. On the KA655 module, the arbiter
  selection is via the 20 pin console config & display connector.)

  The memory interconnection will be a problem, as the KA630 has one
  megabyte on-board and it expects both itself and the memory to be
  sitting on Q22/CD slots, and it doesn't expect to share the CD. 
  I'll have to take a look at the specs -- if I still have a copy --
  but you're probably going to be limited to the megabyte resident on
  the KA630 module.  (You may be able to use a second BA23 to get a
  second, seperate CD -- but I haven't tried this and don't know if
  it will work.)

  The module can be bootstrapped via shared memory or via an (EE)PROM.
  (You'll need to write your own bootstrap and operating system.)
  
  You will not be able to get OpenVMS SMP running on this system, as the
  internal system design (and specifically the memory subsystem) is not
  fully symmetric -- interprocessor communications is available only via
  shared memory windows and interprocessor doorbells.

  My personal preference for this sort of asymmetric configurtion would
  lean toward the KA640 or the KA660 processors -- those processors have
  integrated DSSI and network connections, as well as more on-board memory.

  If you want to pursue this task, you'll need a copy of the appropriate
  processor technical manual.  It should have the PROM footprint and the
  arbiter selection switch settings, as well as most of the information
  you'll need about the module to write your own operating system for it.

