In article <Ct9pq2.Hv@icdc.delcoelect.com> tvmyers@koicds01.icdc.delcoelect.com (Thomas V. Myers) writes:
>Malcolm Weir (malc@park.uvsc.edu) wrote:
>
>> Its actually not even smart enough to be a RAID _5_ system: its really a
>> RAID _4_ system with cache and a CPU and firmware (Coo!  New Technology!!!!).
>
>I guess I've been living in a cave, but I've only heard of RAID systems
>numbered 0 (simple disk striping), 1 (mirrored), 3 (dedicated parity) and 5
>(scattered parity).  What's 4 (or 7 for that matter, no flames please)?
>

I'd suggest that maybe *you* haven't been living in a cave, but the rest of
us have  :).

The generic differences between RAID levels 2 & 3 and 4 & 5 is that in
levels 2 & 3 store a single "block" split across all the disks, while
levels 4 & 5 store one "block" entirely on one disk.  Level 2 uses
multiple check disks (read: ECC), while level 3 uses a single parity drive.
Level 4 uses a dedicated parity disk, while level 5 "rotates" parity across
all drives.  Level 6 uses a two dimensional parity interleave scheme.

Note that the above definitions are fairly hopelessly vague and woolly,
since they omit to define what a "block" actually might be (host block,
sector, or what), and omit some obvious arrangements.  For example,
what do you call a RAID 4 scheme with ECC?  A RAID 3 scheme with
rotating parity? A combnation of levels 0 and 1?

This woolliness has lead to some interesting pseudo-definitions: Level 10,
Level 53, and  the most amusing of them all, RAID 7.

Shakespeare's Macbeth accurately described RAID 7 as "full of sound and fury,
signifying nothing!"  (and then the woods began to move).  Well, maybe
he was referring to something else, but the description is apt!

Malc.

