In article <30guuk$f0r@charm.magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu>, 
rschalk@magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu (Rudy A Schalk) writes:

> The mouse recently died on our vaxstation II. I ordered a replacement from
> DEC and was sent a logitech 3 button mouse model # vsxxx-ga. The original
> mouse plugged into the back of the system with a 15 pin d connector. The
> new mouse plugs into the monitor cable with a round connector. I plugged in
> the new mouse and the system does not see it. Is there something that I
> need to set in the operating system to recognize the new mouse, or to tell
> it to look for a mouse connected to the monitor/keyboard cable?

Your original mouse was a DEC VS10X-EA mouse, which only
worked with the original QVSS (VCB01) single-plane graphics
board.   While the later mice (VSXXX-AA & VSXXX-GA) communicate 
using RS-232 type signals, these older mice used TTL signal 
levals, with two signals in quadrature for each axis.   There 
are also individual TTL signal lines for each of the three 
buttons.   In all, there are a total of ten wires inside that 
tiny mouse tail!

As I recall, the VCB01 would recognize which mouse was
connected at power-up time.   If you have not already
done so, I suggest that you simply power down your system
and turn it back on.

Further trivia:  The older mice were purchased by DEC from
Hawley "The Mouse House" in Berkeley, California.   This was
the same mouse that gained fame on the old Xerox Alto workstation,
several years before the first VAXstation.   In fact, if you
open up your dead mouse, you will find Hawley's model number
on the printed circuit board: "x063x".   Hint: read it upside
down.

If you cannot get your new mouse to work with the VCB01,
I suggest that you upgrade to the VCB02 (GPX) graphics
boards.   This should cost you less than $100 from most
used board vendors, but will take up two slots instead of
one.   If you cannot upgrade and cannot find a used equipment
vendor with a VS10X-EA mouse, I can probably find one for
you -- just call or e-mail.

