In article <30rq92$bet@nova.netapp.com>, hitz@nova.netapp.com (Dave
Hitz) writes:
|> 	Bit-wise striping with parity (RAID 3) provides a single
|> 	high-speed data stream, but since all heads seek together, seek
|> 	time becomes a bottleneck for multi-process loads with separate
|> 	I/O streams.

RAID 3 is supposed to be byte-wise striped, not bit-wise, according to
the
original Berkeley papers.  'Only one I/O request may be serviced per
parity disk 
at one time' (I thought this was non-obvious for reads, since a read
does not
use the parity disk.  However, since all disks seek together, there can
be only 
one I/O active per RAID 3 unit - which is the same thing.)

|> 
|> 	Block-wise striping with parity (RAID 4) allows each disk to
|> 	seek separately, reducing seek time for parallel I/O access,
|> 	but the parity disk becomes a bottleneck because it participates
|> 	in every write.
|> 
|> 	Block-wise striping with rotating parity (RAID 5) solves the
|> 	hot parity because it spreads parity over all the disks, thus
|> 	evening up the load.

The Berkeley papers say 'Several reads and writes per parity
disk can be serviced concurrently'.  Since *every* disk is a parity
disk in RAID5, it's not clear what this means.  On average, a read 
needs to access one drive, a write two, and the drives are randomly
chosen; so some clever statistics are needed (it seems to me) to work
out the likely limits in a real situation.  Perhaps they meant to say
'Several reads and writes per RAID 5 unit', which makes better sense.

|> 
|> I disagree with the implication that RAID 5 is necessarily "better"
|> than
|> RAID 4.  I think it makes more sense to stick with RAID 4 and solve
|> the
|> hot-parity problem with better write allocation patterns in the file
|> system.  The benefit is that you can easily add new disks to the end
|> of
|> a RAID 4 array by simply zeroing them.  Expanding a RAID 5 array is
|> tricky.

To use these things properly and get decent write speeds, one wants
to avoid doing read-modify-write on the parity drive.  The only
way to do this is to write in blocks a whole RAID unit wide.  And if
one
does *that*, the functional difference between RAIDs 3, 4 and 5 
disappears anyway.  OK; you can keep a cache image of the parity 
drive and pretend that's avoided the need to do the 'read' part of
'read-modify-write', but that's really no different from claiming that
cache
could abolish *all* your disk I/O - it just makes the read faster,
that's all.

Perhaps one might say 'If you're doing I/O better with RAID 5,
you're not doing it right'...

--
Dick


