In article <773604378snz@jenson.demon.co.uk>, robert@jenson.demon.co.uk (Robert Lapes) writes:
>
>Can anyone help with a StorageWorks question ?
>
>I'm trying to get info' on the HSD30.
>
>I've found out that there are 3 variants with 0,16 and 32 MB cache.

The cache supported in the HSJ40, HSD30 and HSZ40 controllers
is currently a read cache.  I'm not sure I can comment on the
possbility of a write-back cache when the cache memory has a
battery backup.

Using the SET command, the transfer size threshold can be
set.  If a transfer is over the threshold size it won't
be cached by the controller.  For transfers the size of
the threshold or smaller they will be kept in the cache
(think using an LRU algorithm for replacement).

>
>Can anyone quantify the value of the extra cache ?

The affect of the cache depends a great deal on the work
load and the presense of a host cache in front of the
controller.  Some bad work loads might be:

Most anything when the host has it own cache, UNIX systems
being good examples.  What will often happen is that the
controller caches data that the host already has cached.
Worst the host usually has no way to the controller what
is bad to cache.

A write intensive work load where very data is ready back
soon.  More data than the cache can old is written, continually
flushing the oldest data.  Then when the application gets back
around to reading it, it isn't in the cache.

Sequential reading of large datasets.  The cache can't hold
all the data, so it keeps getting flushed.  This is where setting
the threshold may have biggest payback.  Such applications usually
do large reads.  If you can set the threshold so that it doesn't try
to cache these reads, then the cache kept in the cache may be
useful to a different work load.

Good work loads:

Lots of write followed by read.  The data will be kept in the
cache after being written, then when read soon afterward will
be satisifed from the cache.

Very hot files with lots of reads.  If the files are small
they'll fit in the cache or their busiest parts will fit 
in the cache, requiring few real reads.

