In article <CsKsAo.MA5@umdnj.edu>,
Ravi Subramaniam <subraman@soma.UMDNJ.EDU> wrote:
>1. The console seems to "accumulate" memory.  We have a few
>xterms connected via ethernet to our DECstation 5000. The problem appears
>if x-window based applications like wordperfect 5.1 or
>dxpsview are run for a while.  The amount of memory
>that is used by "XWs", that is the X server, keeps accumulating
>as though something is not getting cleared.  This remains so
>even after the applications are no longer running, and eventually
>clogs up the machine. It can then be cleared only by killing the
>process that shows up under "bc :0 (Xws)" on a listing of processes.
>This of course, will kill the console window.

All X servers do this - the memory used by the server is never given
back to the kernel.  malloc will try to reuse the memory if it can,
so the X server usually reaches some point where it maintains a pretty
constant memory usage.  You can add '-once' to the line in /etc/ttys
that starts the server so that the server is restarted each time someone
logs out so it gives back the memory.  We do this on all our DECstations,
and the equivalent xdm incantation on Suns and DEC Alphas.

