|>Now try real*16, qcosd() and qsind() and....yea! You finally get
|>zero even though the float() call should return a real*4 value
|>which is then added to a real*16 value.
|>
|>Surprisingly, the problem is gone as soon as I compile the code
|>with the /nooptimize qualifier (looks like an optimizer-bug, 
|>doesn't it?).

No, it's not.  It's a Run-Time Library bug in OpenVMS AXP V6.1.  The reason it 
matters whether or not you also call sind is that the compiler uses an 
optimized entry point that computes both the sin and cos in a single call.  
This routine has a bug in in when the argument is 90.0.

If you contact your Digital software support center, they will get you a
correction as soon as it is available.  In the meantime, use /NOOPT as
a workaround.
