In article <1994Jun30.142251.22300@cc.usu.edu> hodges@npt.nuwc.navy.mil writes:
>We are trying to find out what the LAT timing parameters are. This is
>in an attempt to avoid potential problems as our "LAT" network grows in
>size.
>

I used to work on the VMS LAT driver.  Here are some answers from the
current lucky guy.  You've probably already heard them from your local
Digital rep.  In the following text, "the customer" is probably YOU:

    The customer may not have to change anything when running LAT over a T1
    link.  As long as the roundtrip delay remains within ~400ms, there
    shouldn't be much of an issue.  When roundtrip delays begin to get
    large and network delays get long, LAT timeouts will probably occur and
    there isn't much tweeking to be done.
    
    The key thing to remember is the LAT timeout period is fixed at 1
    second in all implementations and that can NOT be changed.
    
    If your customer is thinking about something to change to maybe help
    ease network congestion, then there are a couple of things to consider:
    
    1) Increase the circuit timer on master nodes (terminal servers and
    those hosts that allow SET HOST /LAT).  The default is 80ms and maybe
    100 to 120ms might help ease some link congestion.
    
    2) Increase the keepalive timer from 20sec to maybe 40 to 60seconds. 
    This will decrease the frequency of keepalive messages to help keep the
    link clean.
    
    3) Increase the host multicast timers from 60sec to 120 to 180seconds. 
    This will keep the multicast traffic down that has to go through the
    link as well.
    
    There are merits to changing those values but there are also drawbacks:
    
    1) A longer circuit timer means larger delays in character echoes and
    data movement.  Might hinder a good typist.
    
    2) Increasing the keepalive timer will mean a longer period of time for
    the host to notice when the server actually does fail.
    
    3) A larger multicast interval means a longer period of time before the
    terminal servers realize host systems are available.
    
    Another parameter that may need to be changed is the retransmit limit
    (not retransmit interval - that's fixed at 1sec).  The retransmit limit
    on servers is (by default) 8.  This means that the server will send the
    same message 8 times before giving up on the host.  This may be
    insufficient for some link glitches.  Increasing the retransmit limit
    to 24 to 30 might help.  There have been some bridge spanning tree
    blackouts that may take 45 seconds so you may find that a retransmit
    limit of 50 to 60 is necessary.
    
===============================================================================

    The LAT architecture even states that LAT assumes the network is a
    local device. The software has been designed and coded with a 1
    second retransmit interval.  On OpenVMS that means that some timer
    fires every second and something happens when that timer fires.

    Sure lots of customers bridge LAT over great distances.  As I
    mentioned earlier, just keep the round trip delay down (~400ms or
    lower) and they should be OK.  A satellite link is a bad idea
    because the round trip delay for such a link is far too close to 1
    second.
   
    Depends on the round trip delays and speed of the links.  LAT can
    work over a 57.6kb link when configured correctly.  However,
    saturate the link and begin violating the round trip delays (or
    dropping packets) and LAT performance will pay a penalty.

    The customer was also wondering if there is a product on the
    market that could wrap up the LAT packets and send them over IP
    (for example) and unwrap them at the destination.

    There is no product that does exactly this but Xyplex and I think
    Cisco have products that will use LAT on the local area, branch
    out over X.25 or TCP to another remote site and then get back to
    LAT on the remote site.  This sort of accomplishes tunneling LAT
    through TCP or X.25.

You might also want to consider using telnet over TCP/IP if you are
going over a wide area network.  Many terminal servers support telnet
as well as LAT these days.

-- 
Carl J. Appellof (cja@chmist.zso.dec.com)
