The main thing you have to keep an eye on when a LAT network gets large
is propogation delay.  LAT has a fixed 1 second retransmit timer (time
in between packet retransmits) so large roud trip delays will cause
excessive retransmits (which will affect LAT performance).  A lot of
the bridge products today allow you to prioritize LAT traffic through
the bridge to help cut down on round trip delays.

On a terminal server, there are only 3 things (that are related to timing)
that you can change:

	1) Circuit timer
	2) Retransmit limit (sort of)
	3) Keepalive timer

The circuit timer governs how often the terminal server will transmit data
(when data is available) to the host.  The larger you make this value, the
less frequently the server sends messages.  By default, the value is 80ms.
This value is good as a study was done back when LAT was invented and it
was found that 100ms delays between key touch and character echo hindered
good typists (that is, those of you with nimble fingers that can type
65 words per minute).  A terminal server does not send data to the host when a
key stroke is made.  Instead, it procrastinates to allow data to be aggregated
first.  This aggregation period is the circuit timer.  On a DECserver 200, the
range for a circuit timer is 30ms to 200ms.  The architectural limit is 10ms to
1000ms.

The retransmit limit is the number of times a LAT message is retransmitted
before the terminal server decides that the host is no longer being social.
By default, the value is 8.  So, if the terminal server sends the same message
to the host 8 times and gets no response, the server will disconnect the
virtual circuit and all users on that terminal server who have connections
to the host.  Each message is retransmitted 1 second apart.  You cannot change
that 1 second interval but you can increase the retransmit limit to allow
LAT to be more tolerant of network dropouts.  On a DECserver 200, the range for
the retransmit limit is 4 to 120 (also the architectural limit).  The smaller
the value, the faster a circuit timer is detected.  The larger the value, the
longer it takes to discover a timeout.

The keepalive timer simply is the period of time the terminal server will
wait before sending a "keepalive" message to the host to see if the host is
still on the network.  By default, this value is 20 seconds.  So, on an idle
virtual circuit. the host and terminal server will exchange a message every
20 seconds to keep the connection alive.  This is present because LAT was
designed with the VAXcluster in mind (or today's VMScluster).  It allows a
terminal server to detect a connection failure to a particular host so that
failover can be performed to another more available host in a cluster.  On a
DECserver 200, the keepalive interval can be 10s to 180s (architectural limit
as well).  A larger keepalive timer simply means a less frequent poll period on
an idle virtual circuit.  It may take longer for node failures to be discovered
on a long keepalive interval during idle periods (and thus, it would take
longer for failover).  If failover occurs, you still have to login to the
system the connection failed over to.

On a host (such as an OpenVMS host), the only timer that you can really change
is the multicast timer.  This is the interval of how frequently the host
announces its presence to the network.  By default, this interval is 60
seconds (range can be 10 to 180 seconds).  This is the frequency which a host
announces itself to the network and it is these "service announcement" messages
that the terminal servers use to build a service and node database from.

As a LAT slave, you cannot change the retransmit limit or keepalive timer on a
host.  For OpenVMS, these values are both calculated to be the terminal
server's keepalive timer multiplied by 5 (so a default 20 second keepalive
timer on a terminal server means a VMS host will use 100 seconds as its
keepalive timer and 100 messages as its retransmit limit before declaring the
terminal server dead).  VMS calculates these values when a LAT connection is
completed between a terminal server and the target VMS system.

Note, on OpenVMS V5.5 or higher (or any OpenVMS AXP system), it is possible to 
change a "circuit timer", "keepalive timer" or "retransmit limit".  However,
you must keep in mind that these parameters on the host only come into play if
that VMS system is used as a LAT master (such as when you use the SET HOST
/LAT command).  These parameters only govern outgoing host LAT connections.
Typically, a user making a connection to a host from a terminal server results
in the server as the LAT master and the host as the LAT slave.

>  Also, does anyone know where more detailed protocol info can be found?

There is a publication put out by Digital called LAT Network Concepts (order
number AA-LD84B-TK).  It does not go into too much detail about LAT but should
be enough to get the main principles in layman's terms.

Also, I teach a DECUS seminar called Advanced LAT Concepts (it should be
advertised for the coming fall DECUS).  The seminar discusses the LAT protocol
from start to finish (minus certain details that are only available through
the purchase of a LAT license from Digital) and is geared towards system and
network managers.  My handouts include seminar slides as well as the LAT
Concepts Book and an article about the invention of LAT from a 1986 Digital
Technical Journal that was published for Digital's network products.

Hope this helps.
--
