The RZ23 (and RZ24, RZ55, RZ56) all have spinup enable as a bit stored in a non-volatile
memory location.  Thus, to enable spinup on power up, you have to have some program
or diagnostic that can change that bit.  (fortunately, it's always the same bit)

There are several ways to do it:

1:	The field-service diagnostics in a VAXstation can set the bit, but in a rather
	cryptic way (you tell the diagnostics to do something with a tape drive, but it's
	really a disk, but the results are good)  However, you do have to be in 
	field service mode (which requires that you have the little plastic "spoon" 
	jumpers for the serial lines)

2:	DECstation 3100's and 2100's have the capability to set the spinup bit

3:	I've seen a shareware program that runs on PC's that sets the bit, this is 
	only compatible with Adaptec 154x controllers

4:	Many of the Macintosh SCSI tools allow you to set the bit.  (Silverlining 
	for sure, others I believe)

depending on what you have and where it is, one choice may be better than the other, but 
the Macintosh solution is probably the most readily available.


BTW, that is a rather slow disk, especially compared to the other SCSI stuff from Digital 
as well as others these days.



