In article <2vv2ic$cdt@hermes.ridgefield.sdr.slb.com>, 
    				lovell@sdr.slb.com (John Lovell) writes:
> Hi, I'm having trouble getting my Alpha VAX to allow me
> to pop windows onto its screen from other (VAX + UNIX)
> workstations running either DECNET or TCP/IP protocols.
> I have entered the transport/host/username's into
> the Security-Options "pop-up" window, but no luck.

        First, you should verify that  you  have  both DECnet and TCP/IP
    transports   enabled  for  DECwindows.   This  is  done  by  editing
    SYS$MANAGER:DECW$PRIVATE_SERVER_SETUP.TEMPLATE   (and   saving   the
    modifications  to  the  same  location  as  .COM)  to set the symbol
    decw$server_transports to include the  transports  you  want to use,
    e.g.,

        $ decw$server_transports == "DECNET,LOCAL,LAT,TCPIP"

    My guess is that  the  default  turns  on  only the LOCAL transport,
    which  would  explain  why  you haven't been able  to  display  from
    DECnet-connected nodes.

        Once you've verified  that  you've  got  both  DECnet and TPC/IP
    transports  activated  (this will take restarting DECwindows,  which
    may in turn require rebooting the system, although an "@DECW$STARTUP
    RESTART" may be sufficient...), then  you  can  investigate  getting
    your  security options right.  You should be aware that since TCP/IP
    does _not_ propagate a userid  (for  Xwindows) that you must specify
    an  asterisk "*" for the Username field in the Security Options menu
    for TPCIP nodes.
 
> Can I just delete the security-options altogether and
> issue a flag somewhere to let anyone draw to my screen
> (the VAX equivalent of XHOST +).

        I'm not aware of such a crude way to do it. :-)  However, if you
    specify "* * DECNET" and "* *  TCPIP"  (where  the two *'s go in the
    Node  and  Username  fields), that pretty much opens you up  to  the
    world...

        -Ken
