[Just to clarify a few things]

  The 16-port version has limited modem control, which you can select to be
either CTS-DSR-RTS-DTR or RI-DCD-DSRS-DTR. We've successfully used the first
set with the DS700's DSR rewired to the modem's CD for SLIP as well as normal
access.

> Can't help you there.  Mine all talk to a BSDI/386 box.  They're
> great terminal servers.  You can probably find the 8 port models
> for 7-900 bucks and the 16-port models for 1000-1200 bucks.  You'll
> also want to upgrade the RAM to 5mb and make sure you can get the
> software for them for your Sun (they don't boot off flash RAM and
> need a host with the software to kickstart).

  Those are prices for the discontinued older versions (1MB RAM, no flash) from
the usual used equipment places. DEC still sells the newer versions (2MB RAM,
flash-ready) for much higher prices. A few comments about the flash versions -
"flash-ready" means you can add a flash card, not that it already has one. A
flash boot takes longer than a netboot, and when the server crashes, if it 
booted from flash it can't save a crash dump, so it'll be hard to get DEC to
fix the bug. Lastly, the software has frequent updates (well, not through the
official distribution, but I get one every few weeks from the support center),
so if you boot from flash you should have a fall-back host to netboot the up-
dated image from (so you can write it to flash).

  DEC sells a media kit for "generic Unix" on 150MB 1/4" tape which can be used
for Suns, HP, etc. However, if you already have bootp/tftp all you really need
is the load image, so if you have a VMS system (for example) somewhere around,
you can FTP the load image to your Sun, assuming you have the distribution CD's
for the VMS version (DEC servers are licensed by server, not by load host, so
it's Ok to copy the load image within the same organization).

