In article <2v1r06$hle@agate.berkeley.edu>, avk@hafnium.cchem.berkeley.edu
(Tony Konashenok) writes:
|>Is it possible to make a two-headed VS II by using one VCB02 and one VCB01?
|>If yes, will they be two separate terminals, or two screens controlled by
|>one keyboard?

    The following assumes OpenVMS VAX and  DECwindows (or DECwindows
    Motif), and all appropriate software licenses...

    Possible?  Yes.  Supported?  Donno.  See the warnings at the end!

    If this is the BA23, you may need to pull out some other boards
    (and adjust CSRs accordingly) to free up slots or Q-bus loads
    to make room for the second VCB.

    When configured, it will appear as one workstation with a screen
    area spanning the two displays.

    One has to do configure the VCB01 CSR to a non-standard address
    to prevent the system from autoconfiguring it -- the MicroVAX II
    prefers the VCB01 over the VCB02, and you will likely want the
    VCB02 as the primary device. 

    If CSR 777300 isn't in use on your system (the VCB01 defaults to
    777200), you can use that.  The CSR is encoded on the 8-switch
    DIP pack at E48.  777200 is 1:On,  2:On, 3:On, 4:On, 5:Off, 6:On,
    7:Off; set 7:on to switch to 777300.  On the 4-switch DIP pack,
    set the switches to 1:Off, 2:On, 3:On, 4:On.

    (You may also need to adjust the CSR and/or vector settings on
    various other devices installed in the Q-bus.)

    Edit SYS$MANAGER:DECW$PRIVATE_SERVER_SETUP.COM to connect the
    VCB01.  Add in something like:

    $mcr sysgen connect gca0 /ada=0/csr=%o777300 -
        /vect=%o130/num=2/driver=gcadriver
    $if f$getdvi("gca0","exists")
    $ then
    $   decw$device == "GAA0,GCA0"
    $   decw$color == "T,F"
    $   decw$bitonal == "F,T"
    $ else
    $   decw$device == "GAA0"
    $   decw$color == "F"
    $   decw$bitonal == "F"
    $ endif

    The above uses the VCB02 as the primary, and assumes it assumes
    a color monitor is present on the VCB02.  (If the VCB02 has a
    monochrome monitor, use decw$color == "F,F" instead.)
 
    Remember the limitations of the MicroVAX II when configuring and
    using this system -- physical memory is limited to 16 megabytes,
