If you are referring to the new IBM POWER2 systems, you probably mean
the RS/6000 58H, 590, or 990.  IBM also has a smaller system that uses
the POWER2 chipset with has less cache, memory bandwidth, etc but I
can't remember the number on that one.

>unoptomized and was
>quite buggy therefore not firm enough to do what it can potentially
>do.

      It would be nice if you mentioned the actual models of the
systems mentioned.  I am going to guess that 12:7 ratio is for a DEC
3400 versus an SGI Indigo2 R4400-150?  In my tests, the MIPS R4K is not
quite as good a number cruncher as the DEC Alpha's (MHZ per MHZ...and
the MHZ on the DEC AXP's are usually higher).  Is the HP a 715 or a
735?

     One problem the IBM RS/6000's have are random memory references. 
Due to the memory system design IBM used, cache misses incur a higher
penalty than other systems (IBM does this to increase main memory
bandwidth).  A 14:7 ratio is similar to my experiences with code that
was written for Cray Fortran&preprocessor -- the arrays were being
accessed in "the wrong direction" which effectively made for huge
stride access.  None of the workstations I am familiar with handle
stride access particularly well, but the penalty for cache misses on
the DEC AXP's is much lower than for the IBM RS/6000's.  So on this
application, a DEC AXP 4710 was about twice as fast as a IBM RS/6000
950.  However, RS/6000's used to come with the VAST-II preprocessor
(standard I think...definitely un-bundled in AIX 3.2.5/XLF 3.1) which
was able to fix this "problem" and improve the execution speed by a
factor of 60.  I was not able to get the KAP preprocessor to do these
transformations and so I was not able to run similar transformed code
on the AXP's.
