Marc,
     I think, as a general rule, if is pretty dodgy mixing 'direct' prints 
and 'queued' prints. After all, wot is the point of a queue if you can 
jump it?  ;-)

     As far as i remember it, jobs which are copied to a queue that has a 
queue on it are implicity spooled (to the device that the queue is 
spooled to) but they don't appear in the queue. I would say that if you
are going to use both queued and direct printing, you would be better of
stopping and unspooling the queue prior to any direct prints. (This 
could be done in your application, or you could stop it before you go home
at night).This should remove any contention problems that there may be. 

    As to the 'flow control' going, there are a number of possible causes, 
and so solutions. It could be that, as you suspect, the TTA port is losing
its settings. The correct route is to find what is changing it and stop it! 
The 'cludge' route is to insert a line into your program which sets up the 
correct TTA settings prior to the copy. (obviously only with the device 
unspooled). You should be able to find the correct setting in your system
startup file (or subsiduary).

     The other possible thought is that the flow control problems are 
caused not by the TTA settings by but by the volume of data. In other 
words, are you printing more/bigger stuff overnight that during the day. 
If you are, it could be that the flow control has never been set up right 
and a large print job over runs the buffers. (try changing the speed on your
printer).

      Sorry if this all sounds a bit vague, but you description was not 
very detailed. Hope some of this may be of use,

Ports

