         DECUS U.S. CHAPTER ANNOUNCES RESULTS OF OFFICER ELECTIONS,
                         WELCOMES NEW BOARD MEMBERS


SHREWSBURY, MA - July 1, 1994 -- The Board of Directors of the U.S. Chapter 
of DECUS, the Digital Equipment Computer Users Society, today announced the 
results of Board officer elections which were conducted during a recent 
meeting of the group. Today also marks the start of terms for new Board 
members who were elected during a vote of the Chapter's membership last 
month.

Margaret Knox, associate director of UNIX, VMS, and Microcomputer Services 
at the University of Texas at Austin Computation Center was elected to her 
second term as president of the U.S. Chapter. A member of DECUS since 1977, 
Ms. Knox was first elected to the Board in 1991. Founder of the Chapter's 
Austin and University of Texas Local Users Groups, she was presented the 
DECUS "Outstanding Contribution by an Individual Award" in 1989.

Thomas McIntyre, vice president of Sales at McIntyre Consulting in Concord, 
Massachusetts was elected to his second term as vice president of the 
Chapter. Since joining the Chapter in 1965 Mr. McIntyre has been involved 
with numerous committees including terms as chair of the Biomedical and 
MUMPS Special Interest Groups. A 1993 recipient of the DECUS "Outstanding 
Contribution by an Individual Award", Mr. McIntyre was first elected to the 
Board in 1993.

Board member Sean Welsh, was elected secretary/treasurer of the Chapter. A 
member of the Chapter since 1984, Mr. Welsh has been involved in leadership 
positions with DECUS since 1988. A past chair of the Chapter's former 
Seminars Committee, he was elected to the board in 1993 and has most 
recently served as director of Leadership Relations.

New Board members include: 

Geriann Goeransson, an application programmer/system analyst with Fermi 
National Accelerator Laboratory in Batavia, Illinois. A member of DECUS 
since 1987, Ms. Goeransson is the immediate past editor of DECUS '93 the 
technical journal of the DECUS U.S. Chapter, and is a 1993 recipient of the 
DECUS "Outstanding Contribution by an Individual Award". 

James Sims, a senior programmer analyst at Grumman Data Systems in North 
Charleston, South Carolina. Mr. Sims has been a member of the DECUS U.S. 
Chapter since 1985. He is a long time members of the former Symposia 
Committee and founder of the Costal Empire Local Users Group. 

William Mayhew, president of Village Systems Workshop, Inc. of Natick, 
Massachusetts. A members of the DECUS U.S. Chapter since 1971, Mr. Mayhew 
has served as chair of the Chapter's Business Practices Users Interest 
Group. He has also served as moderator of several conferences on DECUServe, 
the Chapter's electronic conferencing system.

Under the U.S. Chapter's bylaws the Board of Directors is responsible for 
establishing policy and carrying out the mission and goals of the Chapter. 
Elected members serve on the Board for a period of two years. In officer 
elections the Chapter's president is elected for a two year term. All other 
officer positions carry a one year term.

The U.S. Chapter of DECUS, the Digital Equipment Computer Users Society, is 
an Association of Information Technology professionals interested in the 
products, services, and technologies of Digital Equipment Corporation and 
related vendors. The Association's purpose is to promote the unimpeded 
exchange of information, with the goal of helping its members and their 
organizations to be more successful. The Chapter provides members with means 
to enhance their professional development, forums for technical training, 
mechanisms for obtaining up-to-date information, advocacy programs, and 
opportunities for informal disclosure and interaction with professional 
colleagues of like interests.

