Maybe so if you're talking about 3rd party debuggers but for DEC
to support more than one debugger is madness, especially when none of
them approach the quality of what the competition sells (e.g. Codecenter,
SparcWorks, ...). In fact, neither dbx or DECladebug approaches the quality
of the VMS debugger. The implementation of DEC Fuse on top of these is
hard to take seriously because Fuse doesn't know anything about programs.
For example, take the following expression:

	i = bigfunc(a, b, c);

Let's say you want to see the value of 'b' so you click on it. What
gets selected is 'b,' (note the comma) so that when you then click
on the print button you get a syntax error message. Or, let's say
you want to turn off the printing of source code as you single step
since the source code already appears in the top window. Other than
by shrinking the window where the echoing occurs to zero length,
there's no way to stop this. Even the VMS debugger allows you to
turn this kind of thing off.

It's ironic that both the Borland and Microsoft debuggers on PC's
seem to be better displays of technology that what DEC has for OSF/1.

Of course, it isn't only the debugger group that's suffering this
way. Just look the number of C compilers DEC has on OSF/1.


