Why?  Do MIS management think DEC are going belly-up soon?  This is
hardly likely in the short-term --- DEC lack profitabilty, but not
revenue (yet).

3-4 years is probably longer than the economic life of the new VMS
hardware anyway.  If the situation changes in the next few years, you
easily jump ship at anytime.  e.g., just slot out AXP/VMS/Rdb, and
slot in Sparc/Solaris/Ingres or Intel/OS/2/Oracle, or whatever (see
below for my expansion of this point).

> Now tell them you want to do it because Rdb is a wonderful back end (and 
> oh yeah, it only runs on DEC equipment).  There goes another body part.

Of course if you run Rdb, then you need a DEC server to run it on. 
But if such a datbase server meets your price/performance/functionality 
requirements, then what is the problem?  Such a tight integration of
hardware/OS/database has:

  o  no disadvantage to application flexibility: by sticking to
     appropriate interfacing standards, you can change to a different
     back end with no change to your development tools or
     applications.  E.g., The cost would be orders of magnitude less than
     redeveloping your applications.

  o  potential advantages in efficiency and functionality in tightly
     linking database and operating system.  An example of the
     opposite way of doing things is Oracle where the interface
     between the database and VMS is at best
     lowest-common-demoninator,  or at worst plain dumb (for example,
     use of monolithic container files renders much VMS file system
     functionality useless for manipulating your database; up to
     Oracle v5 (!) all images were explicitly based, and hence had to
     be relinked at every VMS upgrade (argghh!)).



