  Problem description
  -------------------

  System configuration :

    DEC 5000 running Ultrix V4.3 with a local file system
    and an NFS file system on a CDC 4680-302 running
    EP/IX 2.1.1 A/B.

  The remote file system is mounted with the command :

  "/shared/host/artas@uxfiles:/home:rw:0:0:nfs:bg,intr,hard:"

  With the local file system no problems were noticed.

  With the remote file system the following (possibly related)
  problems exist :

    1. Crashing Korn shells in new directories
    2. Some files get current time/date during unpack of TAR
       files


  Problem 1 : Crashing Korn shells in new directories
  ---------------------------------------------------

  During the creation of multiple directories with one command
  (e.g. mkdir A B C D) and during the build of a new directory
  tree (e.g. mkdir A; cd A; mkdir B; cd B etc.) the first
  directory is always ok. Some of the other directories however
  cause Korn shell crashes. When a new shell is started in one
  of these directories the system will generate a core dump for
  this shell. Some investigation learnt that the inode number
  of the corrupt directories (ls -lid dirname) is extremely high.
  Furthermore all corrupt directories have the same (big) inode
  number. Switching of attribute cashing really slowed down 
  the system performance, but did not solve the problem. 

  Some more observations :

  - the user home directories are on the remote file system but
    using an account from the local file system only decreased
    the number of corrupt directories (e.g. only D instead of 
    B, C and D)
  - the contents of the dot files in the corrupt directories and
    their parents are correct (=show legal and consistent inode
    numbers)
  - the Korn shell crashes because it compares the inode of the
    directory as available in $PWD with the inode of the dot file
    in the directory itself. The path from $PWD
    (e.g. /home/nlr/post/dirtest/B) delivers the wrong high inode
    number. When the check is not successful the Korn shell starts
    looping in the compare check (bug anyway ?) and finally a
    memory fault occurs and core is dumped.
  - the directories can be repaired by adding a new file to their
    parent directory. After this action the correct inode number
    is shown.
 

                         
  Problem 2 : Some files get current time/date during unpack of
              TAR files
  -----------------------------------------------------------------

  During the unpacking of TAR files on the remote file system some
  of the unpacked files do not get or keep their original time/date
  information, but are timestamped with the current time/date.
  After a move of the files the original timestamps are restored
  and the situation is correct again. The contents of the TAR file
  contain the correct time/date information.


  Please reply to this newsgroup if you know something about this
  problem or contact me via electronic mail (post@nlr.nl).

  Thanks,

     Jacob Post







